BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20170606T23230Z
UID:C3t3pvwL4C-8225471211@spjnetwork.org
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260407T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260407T150000
SUMMARY:Following the Files: How Student Journalists Can Investigate Epstein Connections on Campus
URL:http://us02web.zoom.us/webinar/register/9417744846883/WN_CG2dvd5bSRe2goaXS3ZKzA
DESCRIPTION:The release of the Epstein files has raised questions that reach into institutions across the country\, including college campuses. But how can student journalists effectively\, responsibly and legally pursue those stories? This virtual event is open to any student journalist or educator\, whether you’re just getting started on this topic or already deep into your reporting.

Join the Student Press Law Center and Julie K. Brown\, whose dogged reporting for the Miami Herald helped bring much of the Jeffrey Epstein story to light.
LOCATION:
END:VEVENT





BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20170606T23231Z
UID:0ody6uvHl2-8232010117@spjnetwork.org
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260409
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260409
SUMMARY:Nomination Deadline: Julie Galvan Outstanding Graduate in Journalism Award
URL:http://www.spj.org/julie-galvan-outstanding-graduate-in-journalism-award/
DESCRIPTION:Each of the Society’s campus chapters are encouraged to select one graduate in journalism who is outstanding in his or her class on the basis of character\, service to the community\, scholarship\, proficiency in practical journalism and significant contributions to their SPJ chapter. From this group\, the Society’s leaders will select one graduate who is considered the most outstanding for national recognition.

The Award is named in memory of Julie Galvan\, a former president of the SPJ San Jose State University Campus Chapter.
LOCATION:
END:VEVENT





BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20170606T23232Z
UID:20PsGg5f9j-8232010158@spjnetwork.org
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260409
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260409
SUMMARY:Nomination Deadline: Historic Sites in Journalism Award
URL:http://www.spj.org/historic-sites-in-journalism/
DESCRIPTION:The Society’s Historic Sites in Journalism program honors the people and places that have played important roles in American journalistic history. The program dates back to 1942.

The sites were originally marked with a bronze marker\, and some honorees include: World War II correspondent Ernie Pyle; Benjamin Franklin; William Randolph Hearst; The Associated Press offices in Washington and New York City; Freedom’s Journal\, the first Black newspaper published in the United States; and Supreme Court Justice Hugo Black.
LOCATION:
END:VEVENT





